==English==
[[Image:Waffle iron.JPG|thumb|A waffle iron]]

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[cooking]] [[appliance]], with [[hinged]], [[indented]] [[metal]] [[plate]]s, used to make [[waffle]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Samuel Griswold Goodrich|year=1838|passage=Such a one is very apt to leave his impress upon his scholars, as the '''waffle-iron''' is impressed upon the cake that is baked in it.|page=331|title=Fireside Education|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=n0wXAAAAYAAJ&dq=%22waffle%20iron%22&pg=PA331#v=onepage&q=%22waffle%20iron%22&f=false}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=The Ladies' Garland|volume=5|issue=2|page=36|passage=I remember once of buying a '''waffle-iron''' of him, and when I tried it and found it did not make a pretty pattern on the waffles, I took it back to him to change it: but having no other pattern, he returned me the money as soon as I asked him.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=Fi3mAAAAMAAJ&dq=%22waffle%20iron%22&pg=PA36#v=onepage&q=%22waffle%20iron%22&f=false|title=Mr. Smith|author=Miss Leslie|date=August 20 1841}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Who Put the Devil in Deviled Eggs?: The Fascinating Stories Behind America's Favorite Foods|author=Ann Treistman|year=2011|passage=These batter-based flat cakes gained a new form of popularity in America when Thomas Jefferson returned from his stint in France as a diplomat. In 1789 he presented his friends with a long-handled '''waffle iron'''.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=o9BVnAvry80C&lpg=PA150&dq=%22waffle%20iron%22&pg=PA150#v=onepage&q=%22waffle%20iron%22&f=false}}

